# A 2-limb number times a power of two c: exact unless a product is rounded in the subnormal
# range. It ends with Fast2Sum because the second product, rounded there, can land half a gap from
# the first, and the pair is then no longer normalized. Its exactness when each product is zero or
# at least 2^(p-1) eta in magnitude is not stated: the vocabulary has no such condition yet.
kernel:        mul_pow2_2
input:         x = x0 x1
input:         c
output:        z = z0 z1
value:         x*c
requires:      normalized x
requires:      power_of_two c
ensures:       normalized z
ensures:       error z R c=0 k=2 t=1
min_precision: 2
theorem:       proofs/theories/MulPow2.v mul_pow2_2_correct
source:        both limbs scaled, then Fast2Sum (Milestone 1 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
ieee:          bridge spec
ieee_finite:   abs_le(x*c,-1)
entry:         yes

h        = prod(x0, c)
l        = prod(x1, c)
(z0, z1) = fast_two_sum(h, l)
